    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Discover how web browsers fetch HTML files over HTTP, parse document trees, and display web pages.",
    "content": "# Lesson 01: How the Web Works: Browsers, HTTP Requests & HTML Structure\n\nEvery website you visit - Google, YouTube, your bank - is built from three languages: **HTML** (the structure), **CSS** (the design) and **JavaScript** (the behaviour). In this lesson you will understand what happens between typing an address and seeing a page, and you will write your first HTML page.\n\n## 1. What happens when you open a website?\n1. You type `www.example.com` in the browser (Chrome, Edge, Safari, Firefox).\n2. **DNS** translates the name into an IP address - like looking up a phone number in contacts.\n3. Your browser sends an **HTTP request** to that server: *\"GET me the page / please\"*.\n4. The server sends back an **HTTP response** - a status code plus the HTML file.\n5. The browser reads (parses) the HTML, downloads the CSS, images and JavaScript it mentions, and **renders** the page.\n\n```text\nYou  --(type URL)-->  Browser  --(HTTP GET /)-->  Server\n                      Browser  <--(200 OK + HTML)-- Server\n                      Browser  --(GET style.css, logo.png, app.js)--> Server\n                      Browser  renders the page on your screen\n```\n\n## 2. Common HTTP status codes\n\n| Code | Meaning | You see it when |\n|---|---|---|\n| 200 | OK | The page loaded normally |\n| 301 / 302 | Redirect | The page moved to a new address |\n| 404 | Not Found | Wrong link or deleted page |\n| 403 | Forbidden | You are not allowed to see it |\n| 500 | Server Error | Something broke on the server |\n\n**HTTPS** (with the padlock) is HTTP with encryption - nobody between you and the server can read the data.\n\n## 3. The three languages of the web\n\n| Language | Role | House analogy |\n|---|---|---|\n| HTML | Structure and content | Walls, rooms, doors |\n| CSS | Presentation and layout | Paint, furniture, lighting |\n| JavaScript | Behaviour and interaction | Electricity, switches, the doorbell |\n\n## 4. What does HTML look like?\nHTML is made of **elements**, written with **tags** in angle brackets:\n\n```text\n<p class=\"intro\">Hello world</p>\n ^   ^             ^          ^\n |   attribute     content    closing tag\n opening tag\n```\n\n- **Opening tag** `<p>` starts the element, **closing tag** `</p>` ends it.\n- **Attributes** give extra information: `class=\"intro\"`.\n- Some elements are **empty** (no content, no closing tag): `<br>`, `<img>`, `<hr>`, `<input>`.\n\n## 5. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Your first web page\nClick **Try in Sandbox** to see it rendered:\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <title>My First Page</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>Hello, Web!</h1>\n  <p>This is my <strong>first</strong> web page at Bargisy eSchool.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n### Example 2: Nesting elements\nElements live inside other elements - like boxes inside boxes. Always close the inner one first:\n```html\n<p>Our best seller is <em>Eye <strong>Gel</strong></em> this month.</p>\n```\n\n### Example 3: A tiny page with a link and a line break\n```html\n<h2>Contact</h2>\n<p>Jamjoom Street, Jeddah<br>Saudi Arabia</p>\n<hr>\n<a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org\">Learn more on MDN</a>\n```\n\n### Example 4: Viewing the source of any page\nOn any website press **Ctrl + U** (View Source) or **F12** (Developer Tools) to see the HTML the browser received. This is one of the best ways to learn.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> HTML is **not** a programming language - it's a **markup** language. It describes *what things are* (a heading, a paragraph, a link), not *how to calculate* something.\n\n## 6. Common mistakes\n- **Forgetting to close tags**: `<p>Hello` - browsers try to fix it, but results get unpredictable.\n- **Wrong nesting**: `<p><strong>Hi</p></strong>` - close in reverse order: `<p><strong>Hi</strong></p>`.\n- **Typing curly quotes** from Word (`\"like this\"`) in attributes - use straight quotes `\"`.\n\n## 7. Hands-on Student Challenge\nCreate a page that contains:\n1. The title \"About Me\" in the browser tab.\n2. A main heading with your name.\n3. A paragraph about your job with one word in bold and one in italics.\n4. A horizontal line, then a link to `https://www.wikipedia.org`.\n\n:::solution\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <title>About Me</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>Mohammed Ahmed</h1>\n  <p>I work as a <strong>sales manager</strong> and I am learning <em>web development</em> at Bargisy eSchool.</p>\n  <hr>\n  <a href=\"https://www.wikipedia.org\">Visit Wikipedia</a>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- The browser sends an HTTP request; the server returns HTML that the browser renders.\n- HTML = structure, CSS = design, JavaScript = behaviour.\n- Elements use opening/closing tags, can have attributes, and must be nested correctly.\n",
