    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Configure essential head metadata for responsive mobile rendering and character encoding.",
    "content": "# Lesson 03: The head Tag: Title, Meta Charset, and Viewport for Mobile Devices\n\nThe `<head>` is invisible, but it controls important things: the tab title, the character encoding, how the page looks on phones, and links to CSS.\n\n## 1. The `<title>`\nShown in the browser tab, in bookmarks and as the blue headline in Google results.\n```html\n<title>Eye Care Products | Bargisy Pharmacy</title>\n```\nGood titles are **unique per page**, about 50-60 characters, with the most important words first.\n\n## 2. `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">`\nTells the browser how to decode text. UTF-8 supports every language - Arabic, emoji, accents. Without it you may see broken characters like `Ã©` or `???`.\n\n## 3. The viewport meta tag\n```html\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n```\nWithout it, phones pretend to be a 980px-wide desktop and shrink the page - text becomes tiny. With it, the page width equals the phone's width. **Every modern page needs this line.**\n\n## 4. Other common head elements\n\n| Element | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `<meta name=\"description\" content=\"...\">` | Summary shown under the title in Google |\n| `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">` | Loads a CSS file |\n| `<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.png\">` | The small tab icon |\n| `<style> ... </style>` | CSS written directly in the page |\n| `<script src=\"app.js\" defer></script>` | Loads JavaScript |\n\n## 5. Worked examples\n### Example 1: A complete, professional head\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n  <meta name=\"description\" content=\"Daily eye care tips and product guides.\">\n  <title>Eye Care Tips | Bargisy eSchool</title>\n  <style>\n    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 24px; }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>Eye Care Tips</h1>\n  <p>Look away from your screen every 20 minutes.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n### Example 2: Seeing the difference charset makes\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <title>اختبار</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <p>العربية ✓ — émojis 👁️ — symbols € ﷼</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\nAll characters display correctly because of UTF-8.\n\n### Example 3: Viewport test\nOpen any page without the viewport tag on your phone - it looks zoomed out. Add the tag and refresh - it fits. In Chrome desktop, press **F12** then the phone icon (**Ctrl + Shift + M**) to simulate a phone.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> The `charset` meta should be within the **first 1024 bytes** of the page - put it right after `<head>`.\n\n## 6. Common mistakes\n- **Same title on every page** - bad for users with many tabs and for search engines.\n- **Forgetting the viewport tag** - the site looks tiny on phones.\n- **Putting `<meta>` inside `<body>`** - it belongs in the head.\n\n## 7. Hands-on Student Challenge\nWrite the head for a product page \"Hydrating Eye Gel\" for a pharmacy called \"Al Shifa\". Include charset, viewport, a description of under 155 characters, a good title, and an internal style that sets the font to Arial. Add an `<h1>` in the body.\n\n:::solution\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n  <meta name=\"description\" content=\"Hydrating Eye Gel relieves dry, tired eyes for up to 8 hours. Available at Al Shifa Pharmacy with fast delivery in Jeddah.\">\n  <title>Hydrating Eye Gel | Al Shifa Pharmacy</title>\n  <style>\n    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>Hydrating Eye Gel</h1>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `<title>` appears in tabs, bookmarks and search results.\n- `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">` makes every language display correctly.\n- The viewport meta tag is essential for mobile-friendly pages.\n",
