    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Structure document content using proper heading levels for accessibility and SEO.",
    "content": "# Lesson 04: Headings (h1 to h6) & Paragraphs (p) with Typographic Hierarchy\n\nHeadings and paragraphs are the backbone of every page. Used correctly, they make content easy to scan for people, screen readers and search engines.\n\n## 1. Six levels of headings\n```html\n<h1>Heading level 1 - page title</h1>\n<h2>Heading level 2 - main section</h2>\n<h3>Heading level 3 - sub-section</h3>\n<h4>Heading level 4</h4>\n<h5>Heading level 5</h5>\n<h6>Heading level 6</h6>\n```\nBrowsers make `h1` biggest and `h6` smallest, but **the level is about importance, not size** - you control size with CSS.\n\n## 2. The heading hierarchy - like a book's table of contents\n```text\nh1  Annual Sales Report               (one per page)\n  h2  Western Region\n    h3  Jeddah\n    h3  Makkah\n  h2  Central Region\n    h3  Riyadh\n```\nRules:\n- **One `h1` per page** - the main topic.\n- **Don't skip levels** going down (h2 then h4) - it confuses screen readers.\n- Never pick a heading just because you want big text.\n\n## 3. Paragraphs\n```html\n<p>A paragraph is a block of text. Browsers add space above and below it.</p>\n```\nUse `<br>` only for line breaks that are part of the content (addresses, poems) - not for spacing.\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: A structured article\n```html\n<h1>Guide to Dry Eye</h1>\n<p>Dry eye is common among people who use screens for long hours.</p>\n\n<h2>Symptoms</h2>\n<p>Burning, redness and blurred vision that improves with blinking.</p>\n\n<h2>Daily Habits</h2>\n<h3>At the office</h3>\n<p>Follow the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look 20 feet away for 20 seconds.</p>\n<h3>At home</h3>\n<p>Use a humidifier and keep the air conditioning away from your face.</p>\n```\n\n### Example 2: An address with line breaks\n```html\n<h2>Visit Us</h2>\n<p>\n  Bargisy Training Center<br>\n  Prince Sultan Road<br>\n  Jeddah 23511\n</p>\n```\n\n### Example 3: Size is controlled with CSS, not heading level\n```html\n<style>\n  .small-title { font-size: 18px; color: #475569; }\n</style>\n<h1 class=\"small-title\">This is still the most important heading</h1>\n<p>It just looks smaller.</p>\n```\n\n### Example 4: The horizontal rule as a thematic break\n```html\n<h2>Chapter 1</h2>\n<p>The story begins...</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Chapter 2</h2>\n<p>Years later...</p>\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Install the free **HeadingsMap** browser extension (or use the Accessibility panel in DevTools) to see the outline of any page - it shows instantly if levels are skipped.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Several `h1` elements** for decoration.\n- **Using `<br><br><br>`** to create space - use CSS margin instead.\n- **Bold paragraphs instead of headings** - `<p><b>Symptoms</b></p>` isn't a heading for search engines or screen readers.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\nCreate a page for a \"Monthly Sales Review\" with:\n1. One h1.\n2. Two h2 sections: \"Results\" and \"Next Month\".\n3. Under \"Results\", two h3 sub-sections \"Jeddah\" and \"Makkah\", each with a paragraph.\n4. Under \"Next Month\", a paragraph and an address with line breaks.\n\n:::solution\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <title>Monthly Sales Review</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>Monthly Sales Review - November</h1>\n\n  <h2>Results</h2>\n  <h3>Jeddah</h3>\n  <p>Jeddah reached 112% of target, led by the eye gel range.</p>\n  <h3>Makkah</h3>\n  <p>Makkah reached 94% of target; two key accounts delayed orders.</p>\n\n  <h2>Next Month</h2>\n  <p>The review meeting will be held at:</p>\n  <p>\n    Regional Office<br>\n    Tahlia Street<br>\n    Jeddah\n  </p>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- h1-h6 express importance and structure; use one h1 per page.\n- Don't skip levels; style size with CSS.\n- `<p>` for paragraphs, `<br>` only for meaningful line breaks.\n",
