    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Build adaptable, responsive interfaces across all screen sizes: the viewport meta tag & media queries (@media syntax).",
    "content": "# Lesson 77: The Viewport Meta Tag & Media Queries (@media syntax)\n\n**Media queries** apply CSS only when conditions are true - most often, when the screen is a certain width. Combined with the viewport meta tag, they're the core of responsive design.\n\n## 1. The viewport meta tag (required)\n```html\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n```\nWithout it, phones pretend to be ~980px wide and **media queries for mobile never trigger**.\n\n## 2. Media query syntax\n```css\n@media (min-width: 768px) {\n  /* rules applied when the viewport is 768px or wider */\n  .sidebar { display: block; }\n}\n```\nParts:\n- `@media` - the at-rule.\n- Optional **media type**: `screen`, `print`, `all`.\n- **Feature** in brackets: `(min-width: 768px)`, `(orientation: landscape)`, `(prefers-color-scheme: dark)`.\n- Combine with `and`, list alternatives with commas.\n\n```css\n@media screen and (min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 1023px) { /* tablets only */ }\n@media (max-width: 480px), (orientation: portrait) { /* either condition */ }\n```\n\n## 3. Modern range syntax\nNewer browsers accept a clearer form:\n```css\n@media (width >= 768px) { }\n@media (480px <= width < 1024px) { }\n```\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Changing colours by width (visual test)\n```html\n<style>\n  body { font-family: system-ui; padding: 16px; background: #fee2e2; }\n  .label::after { content: \"Small screen (< 600px)\"; }\n  @media (min-width: 600px) { body { background: #fef3c7; } .label::after { content: \"Medium (600-899px)\"; } }\n  @media (min-width: 900px) { body { background: #dcfce7; } .label::after { content: \"Large (900px+)\"; } }\n</style>\n<h2 class=\"label\"></h2>\n<p>Resize the preview to see the colour and label change.</p>\n```\n\n### Example 2: Showing and hiding elements\n```html\n<style>\n  .menu-full { display: none; }\n  .menu-icon { display: inline-block; }\n  @media (min-width: 700px) {\n    .menu-full { display: flex; gap: 16px; }\n    .menu-icon { display: none; }\n  }\n  nav { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; padding: 12px; background: #1e293b; color: white; font-family: system-ui; }\n  nav a { color: white; }\n</style>\n<nav><strong>Logo</strong><span class=\"menu-icon\">☰</span><div class=\"menu-full\"><a href=\"#\">Home</a><a href=\"#\">Shop</a><a href=\"#\">Contact</a></div></nav>\n```\n\n### Example 3: Layout change\n```html\n<style>\n  .layout { display: grid; gap: 10px; font-family: system-ui; }\n  .layout > * { padding: 14px; border-radius: 8px; }\n  main { background: #e0f2fe; } aside { background: #fef9c3; }\n  @media (min-width: 720px) { .layout { grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr; } }\n</style>\n<div class=\"layout\"><main>Main article</main><aside>Related links</aside></div>\n```\n\n### Example 4: Where to put media queries\nTwo common styles - both fine, be consistent:\n```css\n/* A) Next to each component */\n.card { padding: 12px; }\n@media (min-width: 768px) { .card { padding: 20px; } }\n\n/* B) Grouped at the end of the file by breakpoint */\n@media (min-width: 768px) {\n  .card { padding: 20px; }\n  .nav { display: flex; }\n}\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Media queries **don't add specificity**. A rule inside a media query only wins if it comes **after** the base rule - so put media queries below the styles they override.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Missing viewport meta tag** - mobile queries don't fire.\n- **Media queries placed before the base rules** - they get overridden.\n- **Forgetting the parentheses**: `@media min-width: 600px` is invalid.\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Create a page where the heading is 1.5rem by default and 2.5rem from 800px.\n2. Create a two-column layout (content + sidebar) that appears from 768px; below that, the sidebar stacks under the content.\n3. Add a \"📱 Mobile view\" banner that only shows below 500px.\n\n:::solution\n```html\n<style>\n  body { font-family: system-ui; margin: 0; padding: 16px; }\n  h1 { font-size: 1.5rem; }\n  .layout { display: grid; gap: 12px; }\n  .layout > * { padding: 12px; border-radius: 8px; background: #f1f5f9; }\n  .mobile-banner { display: none; background: #fde68a; padding: 8px; border-radius: 8px; }\n  @media (min-width: 768px) { .layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr 260px; } }\n  @media (min-width: 800px) { h1 { font-size: 2.5rem; } }\n  @media (max-width: 499px) { .mobile-banner { display: block; } }\n</style>\n<div class=\"mobile-banner\">📱 Mobile view</div>\n<h1>Responsive Page</h1>\n<div class=\"layout\"><main>Content</main><aside>Sidebar</aside></div>\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- The viewport meta tag is required for responsive pages.\n- `@media (min-width: X) { ... }` applies rules conditionally; combine with and / commas.\n- Media queries don't add specificity - place them after base styles.\n",
