    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Discover how JavaScript executes inside browser engines (V8, SpiderMonkey) and turns static HTML/CSS into live apps.",
    "content": "# Lesson 01: Introduction to JavaScript: The Engine of the Interactive Web\n\n**JavaScript (JS)** is the programming language of the web. HTML gives a page structure, CSS makes it look good, and JavaScript makes it **do things**: react to clicks, validate forms, calculate totals, load data and update the page without reloading.\n\n## 1. What JavaScript can do\n- Respond to user actions (clicks, typing, scrolling).\n- Change page content and styles instantly.\n- Validate forms before sending.\n- Fetch data from servers (weather, prices, reports).\n- Store data in the browser (to-do lists, settings).\n- Run outside the browser too: servers (Node.js), mobile apps, desktop apps, even Excel add-ins.\n\n## 2. Where it runs\nEvery browser has a **JavaScript engine** (Chrome: V8, Firefox: SpiderMonkey, Safari: JavaScriptCore) that reads your code and runs it. No installation needed - the browser is your development environment.\n\n## 3. Your first lines of JavaScript\nClick **Try in Sandbox**, then look at the **console** under the preview:\n```javascript\nconsole.log(\"Hello, JavaScript!\");\nconsole.log(5 + 7);\nconsole.log(\"Today is\", new Date().toDateString());\n```\n`console.log()` prints values to the developer console - your main tool for checking what code does.\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: A tiny calculation\n```javascript\nconst price = 45;\nconst quantity = 3;\nconst vat = 0.15;\nconst total = price * quantity * (1 + vat);\nconsole.log(\"Total with VAT:\", total);   // 155.25\n```\n\n### Example 2: Making a page interactive\n```html\n<button id=\"greet\">Click me</button>\n<p id=\"message\">Waiting for a click...</p>\n<script>\n  document.getElementById(\"greet\").addEventListener(\"click\", function () {\n    document.getElementById(\"message\").textContent = \"Hello! You clicked at \" + new Date().toLocaleTimeString();\n  });\n</script>\n```\n\n### Example 3: A live character counter\n```html\n<textarea id=\"note\" rows=\"3\" cols=\"40\" placeholder=\"Type a note...\"></textarea>\n<p><span id=\"count\">0</span> / 140 characters</p>\n<script>\n  const note = document.getElementById(\"note\");\n  note.addEventListener(\"input\", function () {\n    document.getElementById(\"count\").textContent = note.value.length;\n  });\n</script>\n```\n\n### Example 4: Changing styles\n```html\n<p id=\"status\" style=\"padding:8px;border-radius:6px;background:#e2e8f0\">Status: pending</p>\n<button onclick=\"approve()\">Approve</button>\n<script>\n  function approve() {\n    const s = document.getElementById(\"status\");\n    s.textContent = \"Status: approved ✔\";\n    s.style.background = \"#dcfce7\";\n  }\n</script>\n```\n\n## 5. JavaScript vs Java\nDespite the name, JavaScript has **nothing to do with Java** - the name was a 1995 marketing decision. The official standard is called **ECMAScript** (ES); \"ES6\" (2015) introduced most of the modern features you'll learn.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Open the console on any website with **F12** (or Ctrl + Shift + J) and type `2 + 2` then Enter - the console is also a calculator and a playground.\n\n## 6. Common mistakes\n- **JavaScript is case-sensitive**: `console.Log` doesn't exist; `console.log` does.\n- **Mismatched quotes or brackets** - every `(`, `{`, `\"` needs its partner.\n- **Forgetting to look at the console** when nothing happens - errors appear there in red.\n\n## 7. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Log your name, your city and the result of 365 × 24 (hours in a year) to the console.\n2. Create a page with a button \"Show tip\" that changes a paragraph's text to \"Drink water regularly!\" when clicked.\n\n:::solution\n```javascript\nconsole.log(\"Mohammed\");\nconsole.log(\"Jeddah\");\nconsole.log(\"Hours in a year:\", 365 * 24);   // 8760\n```\n```html\n<button id=\"tipBtn\">Show tip</button>\n<p id=\"tip\">Click the button for today's tip.</p>\n<script>\n  document.getElementById(\"tipBtn\").addEventListener(\"click\", function () {\n    document.getElementById(\"tip\").textContent = \"Drink water regularly!\";\n  });\n</script>\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- JavaScript adds behaviour: reacting to users, changing pages, working with data.\n- Every browser has a JS engine; the console (F12) shows output and errors.\n- `console.log()` is your first debugging tool.\n",
    "tags": "JavaScript, Front-End, Lesson 01, Coding, Beginner",
