    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Master fundamental JavaScript mechanics: the spread operator (...) for arrays and objects: shallow cloning & merging.",
    "content": "# Lesson 45: The Spread Operator (...) for Arrays and Objects: Shallow Cloning & Merging\n\nThe **spread operator** (`...`) expands an array or object into its individual items. It's the standard way to **copy**, **merge** and **add to** arrays and objects without changing the originals.\n\n## 1. Spreading arrays\n```javascript\nconst jeddah = [\"Ahmed\", \"Omar\"];\nconst makkah = [\"Sara\"];\nconst all = [...jeddah, ...makkah, \"Lina\"];\nconsole.log(all);                         // [\"Ahmed\", \"Omar\", \"Sara\", \"Lina\"]\nconst copy = [...jeddah];                 // shallow copy\nconsole.log(Math.max(...[14, 9, 16]));    // 16 - spread into arguments\nconsole.log([...\"ABC\"]);                  // [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"]\n```\n\n## 2. Spreading objects\n```javascript\nconst defaults = { theme: \"light\", fontSize: 16, language: \"en\" };\nconst userPrefs = { theme: \"dark\" };\nconst settings = { ...defaults, ...userPrefs };\nconsole.log(settings);   // { theme: \"dark\", fontSize: 16, language: \"en\" }\n```\nLater properties **override** earlier ones.\n\n## 3. Shallow copies\nSpread copies only the **top level** - nested objects are still shared:\n```javascript\nconst original = { name: \"Report\", meta: { pages: 3 } };\nconst copy = { ...original };\ncopy.name = \"Copy\";          // independent\ncopy.meta.pages = 99;        // shared nested object!\nconsole.log(original.name, original.meta.pages);   // Report 99\nconst deep = structuredClone(original);   // a true deep copy\ndeep.meta.pages = 1;\nconsole.log(original.meta.pages);         // 99 - unaffected\n```\n\n## 4. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Adding without mutating\n```javascript\nconst cart = [\"Gel\", \"Drops\"];\nconst newCart = [...cart, \"Wipes\"];       // add to the end\nconst withFirst = [\"Mask\", ...cart];      // add to the start\nconsole.log(cart, newCart, withFirst);    // cart unchanged\n```\n\n### Example 2: Updating one property immutably\n```javascript\nconst order = { id: 1051, status: \"pending\", total: 4500 };\nconst shipped = { ...order, status: \"shipped\", shippedAt: \"2026-11-17\" };\nconsole.log(order.status, shipped.status);   // pending shipped\n```\nThis pattern is central to frameworks like React.\n\n### Example 3: Updating an item in a list\n```javascript\nconst products = [\n  { id: 1, name: \"Gel\", price: 45 },\n  { id: 2, name: \"Drops\", price: 32 }\n];\nconst updated = products.map(p => p.id === 2 ? { ...p, price: 30 } : p);\nconsole.log(updated[1], products[1]);   // price 30 vs original 32\n```\n\n### Example 4: Removing duplicates with Set\n```javascript\nconst regions = [\"Jeddah\", \"Makkah\", \"Jeddah\", \"Taif\", \"Makkah\"];\nconst unique = [...new Set(regions)];\nconsole.log(unique);   // [\"Jeddah\", \"Makkah\", \"Taif\"]\n```\n\n### Example 5: Merging configuration\n```javascript\nconst baseChart = { type: \"bar\", colors: [\"#2563eb\"], legend: true };\nconst salesChart = { ...baseChart, title: \"Monthly sales\" };\nconst pieChart = { ...baseChart, type: \"pie\", legend: false };\nconsole.log(salesChart, pieChart);\n```\n\n### Example 6: Converting NodeLists to arrays\n```html\n<p>One</p><p>Two</p><p>Three</p>\n<script>\n  const texts = [...document.querySelectorAll(\"p\")].map(p => p.textContent);\n  console.log(texts);   // [\"One\", \"Two\", \"Three\"]\n</script>\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Spread for **copying** creates new arrays/objects - so `===` comparisons can tell when something changed. This is why immutable updates are popular in modern apps.\n\n## 5. Common mistakes\n- **Thinking spread is a deep copy** - use structuredClone for nested data.\n- **Wrong order when merging** - put defaults first, overrides last.\n- **Spreading huge arrays into function calls** - can exceed argument limits (hundreds of thousands of items).\n\n## 6. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Merge `[\"Ahmed\", \"Omar\"]` and `[\"Sara\", \"Lina\"]` and add \"Fahad\" at the end - without changing the originals.\n2. Given `const base = { currency: \"SAR\", vat: 0.15, decimals: 2 }`, create a UAE config with vat 0.05 and currency \"AED\".\n3. From a list of orders, create a new list where order 2's status becomes \"delivered\" (don't mutate).\n4. Make a deep copy of `{ name: \"Q4\", data: { jan: 1 } }` and prove changing the copy doesn't affect the original.\n\n:::solution\n```javascript\nconst west = [\"Ahmed\", \"Omar\"], central = [\"Sara\", \"Lina\"];\nconst team = [...west, ...central, \"Fahad\"];\nconsole.log(team, west, central);\n\nconst base = { currency: \"SAR\", vat: 0.15, decimals: 2 };\nconst uae = { ...base, currency: \"AED\", vat: 0.05 };\nconsole.log(uae);   // { currency: \"AED\", vat: 0.05, decimals: 2 }\n\nconst orders = [{ id: 1, status: \"shipped\" }, { id: 2, status: \"shipped\" }];\nconst updated = orders.map(o => o.id === 2 ? { ...o, status: \"delivered\" } : o);\nconsole.log(updated, orders[1].status);   // original still \"shipped\"\n\nconst report = { name: \"Q4\", data: { jan: 1 } };\nconst copy = structuredClone(report);\ncopy.data.jan = 100;\nconsole.log(report.data.jan, copy.data.jan);   // 1 100\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- `[...arr]` and `{...obj}` copy and merge without mutating.\n- Later properties override earlier ones when merging objects.\n- Spread is shallow - use structuredClone for deep copies.\n",
    "tags": "JavaScript, Front-End, Lesson 45, Coding, Intermediate",
