    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Transform, aggregate, and process data with modern JavaScript: the array reduce() method part 2: grouping objects & flattening data.",
    "content": "# Lesson 49: The Array reduce() Method Part 2: Grouping Objects & Flattening Data\n\n`reduce` can build **any** kind of result - including objects and arrays. This makes it the go-to tool for **grouping**, **counting by category** and **flattening** data, like a PivotTable in code.\n\n## 1. Grouping into an object\n```javascript\nconst sales = [\n  { rep: \"Ahmed\", region: \"Jeddah\", amount: 14000 },\n  { rep: \"Sara\", region: \"Makkah\", amount: 9000 },\n  { rep: \"Omar\", region: \"Jeddah\", amount: 16000 },\n  { rep: \"Lina\", region: \"Taif\", amount: 7000 }\n];\nconst byRegion = sales.reduce((groups, s) => {\n  (groups[s.region] ??= []).push(s.rep);\n  return groups;\n}, {});\nconsole.log(byRegion);   // { Jeddah: [\"Ahmed\", \"Omar\"], Makkah: [\"Sara\"], Taif: [\"Lina\"] }\n```\nThe initial value `{}` is an empty object that gets filled.\n\n## 2. Totals by category\n```javascript\nconst sales = [\n  { region: \"Jeddah\", amount: 14000 },\n  { region: \"Makkah\", amount: 9000 },\n  { region: \"Jeddah\", amount: 16000 }\n];\nconst totals = sales.reduce((acc, s) => {\n  acc[s.region] = (acc[s.region] ?? 0) + s.amount;\n  return acc;\n}, {});\nconsole.log(totals);   // { Jeddah: 30000, Makkah: 9000 }\n```\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Counting occurrences\n```javascript\nconst products = [\"gel\", \"drops\", \"gel\", \"wipes\", \"gel\", \"drops\"];\nconst counts = products.reduce((acc, p) => ({ ...acc, [p]: (acc[p] || 0) + 1 }), {});\nconsole.log(counts);   // { gel: 3, drops: 2, wipes: 1 }\n```\n\n### Example 2: A mini pivot table (region × product)\n```javascript\nconst orders = [\n  { region: \"Jeddah\", product: \"Gel\", qty: 10 },\n  { region: \"Jeddah\", product: \"Drops\", qty: 5 },\n  { region: \"Makkah\", product: \"Gel\", qty: 7 },\n  { region: \"Jeddah\", product: \"Gel\", qty: 4 }\n];\nconst pivot = orders.reduce((acc, o) => {\n  acc[o.region] ??= {};\n  acc[o.region][o.product] = (acc[o.region][o.product] ?? 0) + o.qty;\n  return acc;\n}, {});\nconsole.log(pivot);   // { Jeddah: { Gel: 14, Drops: 5 }, Makkah: { Gel: 7 } }\n```\n\n### Example 3: Flattening nested arrays\n```javascript\nconst weeklyVisits = [[\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\"], [\"Al Dawaa\"], [\"Whites\", \"Lemon\"]];\nconst allVisits = weeklyVisits.reduce((flat, week) => flat.concat(week), []);\nconsole.log(allVisits);   // [\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\", \"Al Dawaa\", \"Whites\", \"Lemon\"]\n```\n(`flat()` does this directly - Lesson 53.)\n\n### Example 4: Indexing by id for fast lookup\n```javascript\nconst customers = [{ id: 7, name: \"Al Nahdi\" }, { id: 9, name: \"Al Dawaa\" }];\nconst byId = customers.reduce((acc, c) => { acc[c.id] = c; return acc; }, {});\nconsole.log(byId[9].name);   // \"Al Dawaa\"\n```\n\n### Example 5: Summary statistics in one pass\n```javascript\nconst values = [12, 45, 7, 30, 18];\nconst stats = values.reduce((s, v) => ({\n  count: s.count + 1,\n  sum: s.sum + v,\n  min: Math.min(s.min, v),\n  max: Math.max(s.max, v)\n}), { count: 0, sum: 0, min: Infinity, max: -Infinity });\nconsole.log({ ...stats, avg: stats.sum / stats.count });   // count 5, sum 112, min 7, max 45, avg 22.4\n```\n\n### Example 6: The modern Object.groupBy\n```javascript\nconst team = [\n  { name: \"Ahmed\", region: \"Jeddah\" }, { name: \"Sara\", region: \"Makkah\" }, { name: \"Omar\", region: \"Jeddah\" }\n];\nif (Object.groupBy) {\n  const grouped = Object.groupBy(team, r => r.region);\n  console.log(Object.keys(grouped), grouped.Jeddah.length);   // [\"Jeddah\", \"Makkah\"] 2\n}\n```\nNewer browsers include `Object.groupBy` - reduce works everywhere.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> When grouping, always `return acc` at the end of the callback. Forgetting it is the most common reduce bug.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Forgetting `return acc`.**\n- **Wrong initial value** (`0` instead of `{}`) for grouping.\n- **Spreading the accumulator every step** on huge arrays - slow; mutate the accumulator inside reduce instead.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\n```javascript\nconst orders = [\n  { customer: \"Al Nahdi\", city: \"Jeddah\", product: \"Gel\", amount: 450 },\n  { customer: \"Al Dawaa\", city: \"Makkah\", product: \"Drops\", amount: 320 },\n  { customer: \"Al Nahdi\", city: \"Jeddah\", product: \"Drops\", amount: 160 },\n  { customer: \"United\", city: \"Jeddah\", product: \"Gel\", amount: 900 },\n  { customer: \"Al Dawaa\", city: \"Makkah\", product: \"Gel\", amount: 225 }\n];\n```\nUsing reduce:\n1. Total amount per customer.\n2. List of customers per city (unique names).\n3. A pivot of city -> product -> total amount.\n\n:::solution\n```javascript\nconst orders = [\n  { customer: \"Al Nahdi\", city: \"Jeddah\", product: \"Gel\", amount: 450 },\n  { customer: \"Al Dawaa\", city: \"Makkah\", product: \"Drops\", amount: 320 },\n  { customer: \"Al Nahdi\", city: \"Jeddah\", product: \"Drops\", amount: 160 },\n  { customer: \"United\", city: \"Jeddah\", product: \"Gel\", amount: 900 },\n  { customer: \"Al Dawaa\", city: \"Makkah\", product: \"Gel\", amount: 225 }\n];\n\nconst perCustomer = orders.reduce((acc, o) => {\n  acc[o.customer] = (acc[o.customer] ?? 0) + o.amount;\n  return acc;\n}, {});\nconsole.log(perCustomer);   // { \"Al Nahdi\": 610, \"Al Dawaa\": 545, United: 900 }\n\nconst customersByCity = orders.reduce((acc, o) => {\n  acc[o.city] ??= [];\n  if (!acc[o.city].includes(o.customer)) acc[o.city].push(o.customer);\n  return acc;\n}, {});\nconsole.log(customersByCity);   // { Jeddah: [\"Al Nahdi\", \"United\"], Makkah: [\"Al Dawaa\"] }\n\nconst pivot = orders.reduce((acc, o) => {\n  acc[o.city] ??= {};\n  acc[o.city][o.product] = (acc[o.city][o.product] ?? 0) + o.amount;\n  return acc;\n}, {});\nconsole.log(pivot);   // { Jeddah: { Gel: 1350, Drops: 160 }, Makkah: { Drops: 320, Gel: 225 } }\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- reduce with `{}` builds grouped objects; with `[]` builds arrays.\n- Perfect for totals per category, counts and pivot-style summaries.\n- Always return the accumulator.\n",
    "tags": "JavaScript, Front-End, Lesson 49, Coding, Intermediate",
    "read_time": 5,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
