    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Master asynchronous data fetching and modern architecture: consuming promises: .then(), .catch(), and .finally() chaining.",
    "content": "# Lesson 92: Consuming Promises: .then(), .catch(), and .finally() Chaining\n\nYou **consume** promises with `.then()` (success), `.catch()` (failure) and `.finally()` (always). Chaining them creates readable step-by-step async flows.\n\n## 1. The three methods\n```text\nsomePromise\n  .then(value => { /* runs on success */ })\n  .catch(error => { /* runs on failure (anywhere above) */ })\n  .finally(() => { /* runs either way - cleanup */ });\n```\n\n## 2. Chaining rules\n- Each `.then()` returns a **new promise**.\n- What you **return** inside `.then()` becomes the next step's value.\n- If you return a **promise**, the chain waits for it.\n- If anything throws or rejects, the chain skips to the next `.catch()`.\n\n## 3. Worked examples\n### Example 1: Passing values down the chain\n```javascript\nPromise.resolve(100)\n  .then(price => price * 3)             // 300\n  .then(subtotal => subtotal * 1.15)    // 345\n  .then(total => console.log(\"Total:\", total.toFixed(2)));\n```\n\n### Example 2: Sequential async steps\n```javascript\nconst wait = (ms, v) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms));\nconst login = () => wait(200, { user: \"sara\", token: \"abc123\" });\nconst loadProfile = token => wait(200, { name: \"Sara Ali\", region: \"Makkah\" });\nconst loadSales = region => wait(200, [9000, 11000, 10500]);\n\nlogin()\n  .then(session => { console.log(\"Logged in\"); return loadProfile(session.token); })\n  .then(profile => { console.log(\"Profile:\", profile.name); return loadSales(profile.region); })\n  .then(sales => console.log(\"Total sales:\", sales.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0)))\n  .catch(err => console.error(\"Error:\", err.message))\n  .finally(() => console.log(\"Done (success or failure)\"));\n```\n**Return** each next promise - otherwise the chain doesn't wait for it.\n\n### Example 3: One catch for the whole chain\n```javascript\nconst step = (name, fail = false) => new Promise((resolve, reject) =>\n  setTimeout(() => fail ? reject(new Error(`${name} failed`)) : resolve(name), 100));\n\nstep(\"Validate order\")\n  .then(() => step(\"Charge card\", true))     // this fails\n  .then(() => step(\"Send email\"))            // skipped\n  .then(() => console.log(\"All good\"))       // skipped\n  .catch(err => console.log(\"Caught:\", err.message))   // Caught: Charge card failed\n  .finally(() => console.log(\"Hide spinner\"));\n```\n\n### Example 4: Recovering inside the chain\n```javascript\nconst getCachedRate = () => Promise.reject(new Error(\"Cache empty\"));\ngetCachedRate()\n  .catch(() => 3.75)                       // fall back to a default value\n  .then(rate => console.log(\"USD rate:\", rate));\n```\nA `.catch()` that returns a value lets the chain continue normally.\n\n### Example 5: finally for UI cleanup\n```html\n<button id=\"load\">Load report</button> <span id=\"spin\"></span>\n<p id=\"out\"></p>\n<script>\n  const fakeLoad = () => new Promise((res, rej) => setTimeout(() => Math.random() > 0.3 ? res(\"Report: 48,000 SAR\") : rej(new Error(\"Server busy\")), 800));\n  document.getElementById(\"load\").addEventListener(\"click\", e => {\n    const btn = e.currentTarget;\n    btn.disabled = true;\n    document.getElementById(\"spin\").textContent = \"⏳ Loading...\";\n    fakeLoad()\n      .then(text => document.getElementById(\"out\").textContent = text)\n      .catch(err => document.getElementById(\"out\").textContent = \"❌ \" + err.message)\n      .finally(() => { btn.disabled = false; document.getElementById(\"spin\").textContent = \"\"; });\n  });\n</script>\n```\nClick several times - it randomly succeeds or fails, and the button is always re-enabled.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Put **one `.catch()` at the end** of a chain, and use **`.finally()`** for things that must happen no matter what - hiding spinners, re-enabling buttons, closing connections.\n\n## 4. Common mistakes\n- **Forgetting to return** a promise inside then - the chain races ahead.\n- **Nesting .then inside .then** - recreates callback hell; return and chain instead.\n- **No catch at the end** - unhandled rejections.\n\n## 5. Hands-on Student Challenge\nUsing `const wait = (ms, v) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms));`:\n1. Chain: get a customer (`{ id: 7, name: \"Al Nahdi\" }`), then their orders (`[4500, 3100]`), then log \"Al Nahdi: 7,600 SAR\".\n2. Add a step that throws an Error if the total is above 5,000 (\"Credit limit exceeded\") and catch it.\n3. Log \"Check complete\" in finally.\n\n:::solution\n```javascript\nconst wait = (ms, v) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms));\nconst getCustomer = () => wait(150, { id: 7, name: \"Al Nahdi\" });\nconst getOrders = id => wait(150, [4500, 3100]);\n\nlet customerName;\ngetCustomer()\n  .then(c => { customerName = c.name; return getOrders(c.id); })\n  .then(orders => {\n    const total = orders.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);\n    console.log(`${customerName}: ${total.toLocaleString()} SAR`);   // Al Nahdi: 7,600 SAR\n    if (total > 5000) throw new Error(\"Credit limit exceeded\");\n    return total;\n  })\n  .then(total => console.log(\"Order approved for\", total))           // skipped\n  .catch(err => console.log(\"Stopped:\", err.message))\n  .finally(() => console.log(\"Check complete\"));\n```\n:::\n\n## Key takeaways\n- then handles success, catch handles failure, finally always runs.\n- Return values (or promises) from then to pass them down the chain.\n- One catch at the end handles errors from every step.\n",
    "tags": "JavaScript, Front-End, Lesson 92, Coding, Advanced",
    "read_time": 5,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
    "status": "published",
    "views": 154,
    "cover_gradient": "linear-gradient(135deg, #78350f, #f59e0b)",
